a-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Alice</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Bob</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAlice and Bob each attempted the math test five times, and their scores are represented in the table above. The mean score for Bob's attempts was $0.5$ greater than the mean score for Alice's attempts. What is the value of $x$?\n\n$x =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186217649 1071 "| **Alice** | $4.2$ | $3.8$ | $3.2$ | $4.0$ | $4.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Bob** | $x$ | $4.6$ | $4.0$ | $3.8$ | $4.8$ |\n\nAlice and Bob each attempted the math test five times, and their scores are represented in the table above. The mean score for Bob's attempts was $0.5$ greater than the mean score for Alice's attempts. What is the value of $x$?\n\n$x =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186217747 1071 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12 - 14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15 - 16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17 - 18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19 - 20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21 - 22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe frequency table above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. What is the smallest possible value of the range?\n\nThe smallest possible range is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186217747 1071 "| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $12 - 14$ | $3$ |\n| $15 - 16$ | $5$ |\n| $17 - 18$ | $4$ |\n| $19 - 20$ | $2$ |\n| $21 - 22$ | $3$ |\n\nThe frequency table above shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. What is the smallest possible value of the range?\n\nThe smallest possible range is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218214 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate the mean of a data set from a histogram.\n\nFor example, what is the mean of the dataset represented by the histogram below?\n\n![](images/e2/e29fd1063c8dc137c7620539361838ffc038072dabb815cd7b9e539527c35bc6.png)\n\nTo find the mean of a dataset from a histogram, we first convert it into a frequency table.\n\nOn a histogram, the horizontal axis represents the values in the dataset, and the vertical axis indicates the frequency of each value in the dataset.\n\nIn our case, placing the data from the given histogram in a frequency table, we obtain the following.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo calculate the mean of a dataset from a frequency table, we multiply each value by its frequency, add the results, and divide by the total frequency.\n\nSo, we now extend the table to include a third column for the product of the values and their corresponding frequencies:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value ($x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency ($f_x$)</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x \\cdot f_x$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$2 \\cdot 3 = 6\\phantom{1}\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$3 \\cdot 1 = 3\\phantom{1}\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$4 \\cdot 2 = 8\\phantom{1}\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$5 \\cdot 3 = 15\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$6 \\cdot 2 = 12\\qquad$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the mean of the dataset is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{6 + 3 + 8 + 15 + 12}{3 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{44}{11} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218214 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate the mean of a data set from a histogram.\n\nFor example, what is the mean of the dataset represented by the histogram below?\n\n![](images/e2/e29fd1063c8dc137c7620539361838ffc038072dabb815cd7b9e539527c35bc6.png)\n\nTo find the mean of a dataset from a histogram, we first convert it into a frequency table.\n\nOn a histogram, the horizontal axis represents the values in the dataset, and the vertical axis indicates the frequency of each value in the dataset.\n\nIn our case, placing the data from the given histogram in a frequency table, we obtain the following.\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ |\n\nTo calculate the mean of a dataset from a frequency table, we multiply each value by its frequency, add the results, and divide by the total frequency.\n\nSo, we now extend the table to include a third column for the product of the values and their corresponding frequencies:\n\n| Value ($x$) | Frequency ($f_x$) | $x \\cdot f_x$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2 \\cdot 3 = 6\\phantom{1}\\qquad$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $3 \\cdot 1 = 3\\phantom{1}\\qquad$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ | $4 \\cdot 2 = 8\\phantom{1}\\qquad$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $5 \\cdot 3 = 15\\qquad$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ | $6 \\cdot 2 = 12\\qquad$ |\n\nTherefore, the mean of the dataset is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{6 + 3 + 8 + 15 + 12}{3 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{44}{11} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218219 1064 "In the bakery example from the beginning of the lesson, we wrote the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nbecause the problem said that the manager wanted\n\n- to buy *at least $36$ items*, and\n- to spend *no more than $\\$180$.*\n\nBoth these phrases lead to *non-strict inequalities* (\n\n$$\n\\geq\n$$\n\n,\n\n$$\n\\leq\n$$\n\n).\n\nBut if the manager instead wants *more than $36$ items*, then the first inequality in the system becomes strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{blue}>} \\hspace{0.222em} 36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe most common phrases and the corresponding inequality symbols are listed in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Phrasing</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Symbol</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">at least, no less than</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\geq$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">at most, no more than</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\leq$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">more than, greater than</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\gt$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">less than, fewer than</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\lt$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218219 1064 "In the bakery example from the beginning of the lesson, we wrote the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\ge  36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nbecause the problem said that the manager wanted\n\n- to buy *at least $36$ items*, and\n- to spend *no more than $\\$180$.*\n\nBoth these phrases lead to *non-strict inequalities* (\n\n$$\n\\geq\n$$\n\n,\n\n$$\n\\leq\n$$\n\n).\n\nBut if the manager instead wants *more than $36$ items*, then the first inequality in the system becomes strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y \\hspace{0.222em} {\\color{blue}>} \\hspace{0.222em} 36 \\\\ 8 x + 2 y \\le  180\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe most common phrases and the corresponding inequality symbols are listed in the table below.\n\n| **Phrasing** | **Symbol** |\n| --- | ---: |\n| at least, no less than | $\\geq$ |\n| at most, no more than | $\\leq$ |\n| more than, greater than | $\\gt$ |\n| less than, fewer than | $\\lt$ |" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218239 1064 "When data is grouped into intervals, it is not possible to determine the median exactly. However, we can *estimate* it. We saw how to estimate the median for grouped data in a previous lesson.\n\nWhen we estimate the median, its actual value might be higher or lower than our estimate. In this lesson, we'll learn how to *bound* the median, which means determining its maximum or minimum possible values.\n\nLet's consider an example. The frequency table below shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20-24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24-28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28-32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32-36$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36-40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40-44$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's determine the *smallest and largest possible values* of the median.\n\nTo estimate the median from grouped data, we first compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20-24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24-28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28-32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32-36$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36-40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40-44$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThus, the total number of values is $21$. Since this is an odd number, the median is the value in the following position:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{21+1}{2}=\\dfrac{22}{2}=11\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine where the $11$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $9$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $12$ positions in total.\n\nTherefore, the $11$th value falls in the fourth interval, which is\n$32 - 36$\nas shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Cumulative Frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20-24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24-28$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$28-32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$32-36$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36-40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40-44$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the *smallest possible value* of the median is $32$, and\n- the *largest possible value* of the median is $36$." 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218239 1064 "When data is grouped into intervals, it is not possible to determine the median exactly. However, we can *estimate* it. We saw how to estimate the median for grouped data in a previous lesson.\n\nWhen we estimate the median, its actual value might be higher or lower than our estimate. In this lesson, we'll learn how to *bound* the median, which means determining its maximum or minimum possible values.\n\nLet's consider an example. The frequency table below shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group.\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $20-24$ | $3$ |\n| $24-28$ | $2$ |\n| $28-32$ | $4$ |\n| $32-36$ | $3$ |\n| $36-40$ | $8$ |\n| $40-44$ | $1$ |\n\nLet's determine the *smallest and largest possible values* of the median.\n\nTo estimate the median from grouped data, we first compute cumulative frequencies.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $20-24$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $24-28$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| $28-32$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| $32-36$ | $3$ | $12$ |\n| $36-40$ | $8$ | $20$ |\n| $40-44$ | $1$ | $21$ |\n\nThus, the total number of values is $21$. Since this is an odd number, the median is the value in the following position:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{21+1}{2}=\\dfrac{22}{2}=11\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine where the $11$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $9$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $12$ positions in total.\n\nTherefore, the $11$th value falls in the fourth interval, which is\n$32 - 36$\nas shown below.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $20-24$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $24-28$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| $28-32$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| $32-36$ | $3$ | $12$ |\n| $36-40$ | $8$ | $20$ |\n| $40-44$ | $1$ | $21$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the *smallest possible value* of the median is $32$, and\n- the *largest possible value* of the median is $36$." 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218241 1064 "OK, so we know how to find the maximum and minimum possible values for the median of a grouped data set. But what about the mean?\n\nThe frequency table below shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. Let's determine the *smallest possible value* of the mean for this data set.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10-14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18-22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22-26$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$26-30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe process for determining the smallest possible value for the mean is similar to how we estimate the mean. However, instead of using the middle value for each group, we use the *smallest possible value* of $x$ for each group.\n\nSo, we begin by recording $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the smallest possible value of $x$ for each group.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{\\textrm{min}}$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{10}-14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{10}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{14}-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{14}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{18}-22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{18}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{22}-26$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{22}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{26}-30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{26}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThen, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Value $(x)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(f)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{\\textrm{min}}$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10-14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4\\cdot 10 = 40$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14-18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3\\cdot 14 = 42$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18-22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5\\cdot 18 = 90$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22-26$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2\\cdot 22 = 44$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$26-30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$26$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6\\cdot 26 = 156$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsmallest possible mean &= \\frac{40 + 42 + 90 + 44 + 156}{4 + 3 + 5 + 2 + 6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{372}{20} \\\\\n&= 18.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe procedure for calculating the *maximum* possible value of the mean is similar. The only difference is that we multiply the *maximum* value of the categorical variable $x$ in each row by its respective frequency. Let's see an example." 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218241 1064 "OK, so we know how to find the maximum and minimum possible values for the median of a grouped data set. But what about the mean?\n\nThe frequency table below shows the number of values in a dataset that fall into each group. Let's determine the *smallest possible value* of the mean for this data set.\n\n| Value $(x)$ | Frequency $(f)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $10-14$ | $4$ |\n| $14-18$ | $3$ |\n| $18-22$ | $5$ |\n| $22-26$ | $2$ |\n| $26-30$ | $6$ |\n\nThe process for determining the smallest possible value for the mean is similar to how we estimate the mean. However, instead of using the middle value for each group, we use the *smallest possible value* of $x$ for each group.\n\nSo, we begin by recording $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the smallest possible value of $x$ for each group.\n\n| Value $(x)$ | Frequency $(f)$ | $x_{\\textrm{min}}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{10}-14$ | $4$ | $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{10}$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{14}-18$ | $3$ | $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{14}$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{18}-22$ | $5$ | $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{18}$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{22}-26$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{22}$ |\n| $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{26}-30$ | $6$ | $\\bbox[3pt, gainsboro]{26}$ |\n\nThen, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.\n\n| Value $(x)$ | Frequency $(f)$ | $x_{\\textrm{min}}$ | $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $10-14$ | $4$ | $10$ | $4\\cdot 10 = 40$ |\n| $14-18$ | $3$ | $14$ | $3\\cdot 14 = 42$ |\n| $18-22$ | $5$ | $18$ | $5\\cdot 18 = 90$ |\n| $22-26$ | $2$ | $22$ | $2\\cdot 22 = 44$ |\n| $26-30$ | $6$ | $26$ | $6\\cdot 26 = 156$ |\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsmallest possible mean &= \\frac{40 + 42 + 90 + 44 + 156}{4 + 3 + 5 + 2 + 6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{372}{20} \\\\\n&= 18.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe procedure for calculating the *maximum* possible value of the mean is similar. The only difference is that we multiply the *maximum* value of the categorical variable $x$ in each row by its respective frequency. Let's see an example." 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218249 1064 "In this lesson, we want to practice connecting tables of values to linear functions, building on what we know about equations and graphs of lines so we can move smoothly between all three representations.\n\nLet’s see an example, where we are given the graph of a function and use it to fill in a table of values that lie on the line.\n\n![](images/b9/b9505f1cb5ca703bdcfbfa9bafa370d094b6b3ef8699e8e83dddee544baa9dc6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=f(x)$ in the coordinate plane, where $f$ is a linear function. Let's fill in the missing values in the table below for points that lie on the line.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the points $(-2, -5)$, $(0, -1)$, and $(3, 5)$ lie on the line.\n\nNow, we’ll analyze each of these points in turn:\n\n- For $(-2, -5){:}$ the function value is $-5$ when $x=-2$.\n- For $(0, -1){:}$ when $x=0$, the corresponding function value is $-1$.\n- For $(3, 5){:}$ when $x=3$, the corresponding function value is $5$.\n\nTherefore, the filled-in table is the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218249 1064 "In this lesson, we want to practice connecting tables of values to linear functions, building on what we know about equations and graphs of lines so we can move smoothly between all three representations.\n\nLet’s see an example, where we are given the graph of a function and use it to fill in a table of values that lie on the line.\n\n![](images/b9/b9505f1cb5ca703bdcfbfa9bafa370d094b6b3ef8699e8e83dddee544baa9dc6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=f(x)$ in the coordinate plane, where $f$ is a linear function. Let's fill in the missing values in the table below for points that lie on the line.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n|  | $-5$ |\n| $0$ |  |\n| $3$ |  |\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the points $(-2, -5)$, $(0, -1)$, and $(3, 5)$ lie on the line.\n\nNow, we’ll analyze each of these points in turn:\n\n- For $(-2, -5){:}$ the function value is $-5$ when $x=-2$.\n- For $(0, -1){:}$ when $x=0$, the corresponding function value is $-1$.\n- For $(3, 5){:}$ when $x=3$, the corresponding function value is $5$.\n\nTherefore, the filled-in table is the following:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $-5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |\n| $3$ | $5$ |" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218250 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to write down a linear function using values in a table. In this lesson, we will focus on analyzing linear functions in tabular form by finding specific values, identifying points on the line, and locating intercepts directly from the table.\n\nLet's see an example.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSome values of the linear function $f$ are shown in the table above. If $x$ can be any real number, let's find the value of $f(8)$.\n\nSince $f$ is a linear function, we know it must be of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = mx + c\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope, and $c$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we find the slope using the two given points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1)=(0, 9)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2)=(3, 3){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 9}{3 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-6}{3} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $-2$.\nThen, we find the $y$-intercept using the point $(0, 9){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 &= -2(0) + c \\\\\n9 &= 0 + c \\\\\nc &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x + 9\n$$\n\nFinally, we find $f(8){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(8) &= -2(8) + 9 \\\\\n&=-16 + 9 \\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this example, we were given the $y$-intercept $(0,9)$, which meant we could deduce $c=9$ immediately. Let's take a look at an example where the $y$-intercept is NOT given." 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218250 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to write down a linear function using values in a table. In this lesson, we will focus on analyzing linear functions in tabular form by finding specific values, identifying points on the line, and locating intercepts directly from the table.\n\nLet's see an example.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $9$ |\n| $3$ | $3$ |\n| $6$ | $-3$ |\n\nSome values of the linear function $f$ are shown in the table above. If $x$ can be any real number, let's find the value of $f(8)$.\n\nSince $f$ is a linear function, we know it must be of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = mx + c\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope, and $c$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we find the slope using the two given points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1)=(0, 9)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2)=(3, 3){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 9}{3 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-6}{3} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $-2$.\nThen, we find the $y$-intercept using the point $(0, 9){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 &= -2(0) + c \\\\\n9 &= 0 + c \\\\\nc &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x + 9\n$$\n\nFinally, we find $f(8){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(8) &= -2(8) + 9 \\\\\n&=-16 + 9 \\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this example, we were given the $y$-intercept $(0,9)$, which meant we could deduce $c=9$ immediately. Let's take a look at an example where the $y$-intercept is NOT given." 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218251 1064 "Sometimes, we may be given a linear function in tabular form and the table itself contains some unknown constants (often called **parameters**). In this lesson, we will learn how to construct the relationship representing the table data.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$k$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$k-1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table shows two points on a line in the $xy$-plane and their corresponding values of $y$, where $k$ is a constant. Let's find the equation that represents the line.\n\nSince the points are on a line, we use two points to find the slope and set up the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the slope using the points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1)=(k,2)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2)=(k-1,-1){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (-1 - 2)/((k - 1) - k) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-3}{-1} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the line in point-slope form using the point $(k, 2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 2 &= m(x - k) \\\\\ny - 2 &= 3(x - k)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange to write the equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 2 &= 3(x - k) \\\\\ny - 2 &= 3x - 3k \\\\\n3k - 2 &= 3x - y \\\\\n3x - y &= 3k - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\n3x - y = 3k - 2\n$$" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218251 1064 "Sometimes, we may be given a linear function in tabular form and the table itself contains some unknown constants (often called **parameters**). In this lesson, we will learn how to construct the relationship representing the table data.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $k$ | $2$ |\n| $k-1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe table shows two points on a line in the $xy$-plane and their corresponding values of $y$, where $k$ is a constant. Let's find the equation that represents the line.\n\nSince the points are on a line, we use two points to find the slope and set up the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the slope using the points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1)=(k,2)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2)=(k-1,-1){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (-1 - 2)/((k - 1) - k) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-3}{-1} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the line in point-slope form using the point $(k, 2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 2 &= m(x - k) \\\\\ny - 2 &= 3(x - k)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange to write the equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 2 &= 3(x - k) \\\\\ny - 2 &= 3x - 3k \\\\\n3k - 2 &= 3x - y \\\\\n3x - y &= 3k - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\n3x - y = 3k - 2\n$$" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218253 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to transform linear functions given in tabular form.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</th></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-14$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFor the linear function $f$, the table shows three values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $f(x)$. If the function $g$ is defined as\n\n$$\ng(x) = f(x) - 7\n$$\n\nlet's use the given information to find $g$.\n\nSince $f(x)$ is linear, it can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ using the points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1)=(-2,10)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2)=(2,2){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (2 - 10)/(2 - (-2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-8}{4} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the $y$-intercept $b$ using the point $(-2, 10){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= -2 \\cdot x + b \\\\\n10 &= -2(-2) + b \\\\\nb &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x + 6\n$$\n\nfrom which it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= f(x) - 7 \\\\\n&= (-2x + 6) - 7 \\\\\n&=-2x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = -2x - 1\n$$" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218253 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to transform linear functions given in tabular form.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n| $10$ | $-14$ |\n\nFor the linear function $f$, the table shows three values of $x$ and their corresponding values of $f(x)$. If the function $g$ is defined as\n\n$$\ng(x) = f(x) - 7\n$$\n\nlet's use the given information to find $g$.\n\nSince $f(x)$ is linear, it can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ using the points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1)=(-2,10)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2)=(2,2){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (2 - 10)/(2 - (-2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-8}{4} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the $y$-intercept $b$ using the point $(-2, 10){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= -2 \\cdot x + b \\\\\n10 &= -2(-2) + b \\\\\nb &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x + 6\n$$\n\nfrom which it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= f(x) - 7 \\\\\n&= (-2x + 6) - 7 \\\\\n&=-2x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x) = -2x - 1\n$$" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218284 1064 "Now, let’s look at the case where we are given a table of values from a line and work backwards to determine the equation of the linear function.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-10$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSome values of $x$ and the corresponding values of $y$ are shown in the table above. There is a linear relationship between $x$ and $y$. Which of the following equations defines this relationship?\n\nSince the relationship is linear, we know it must be of the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + c\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $m$ is the slope, and\n- $c$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we find the slope using two points. For\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (-1, -10)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (2, 2)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (2 - (-10))/(2 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{3} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $4$.\n\nNext, we find the $y$-intercept using one point. For\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (2,2)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4x + c \\\\\n2 &= 4(2) + c \\\\\n2 &= 8 + c \\\\\nc &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation is $y = 4x - 6$." 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218284 1064 "Now, let’s look at the case where we are given a table of values from a line and work backwards to determine the equation of the linear function.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $-10$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n| $5$ | $14$ |\n\nSome values of $x$ and the corresponding values of $y$ are shown in the table above. There is a linear relationship between $x$ and $y$. Which of the following equations defines this relationship?\n\nSince the relationship is linear, we know it must be of the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + c\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $m$ is the slope, and\n- $c$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we find the slope using two points. For\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (-1, -10)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (2, 2)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (2 - (-10))/(2 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{3} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $4$.\n\nNext, we find the $y$-intercept using one point. For\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (2,2)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4x + c \\\\\n2 &= 4(2) + c \\\\\n2 &= 8 + c \\\\\nc &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation is $y = 4x - 6$." 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218312 1064 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$k$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$k-1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, we found that the equation of the linear relationship represented by the data given in the table above is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - y &= 3k - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the line is expressed in terms of $k$. The idea is that for each possible value of $k$, we get a new line. Let's pick a few values of $k$, determine the corresponding line, and then plot these in the plane.\n\n- If $k=0$, our line is\n$3x - y = 3(0) - 2$\nwhich simplifies as\n$3x - y =-2$.\n- If $k=1$, our line is\n$3x - y = 3(1) - 2$\nwhich simplifies as\n$3x - y = 1$.\n- If $k=2$, our line is\n$3x - y = 4$\n\nAnd so on. We can see that each value of $k$ gives a new line, and in this case, all lines are parallel (since they each have a slope of $3$).\n\n![](images/2c/2c928d7e304f264c60838f5ab29bf2269c96005a74fb9710db8bc23946359206.png)" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218312 1064 "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $k$ | $2$ |\n| $k-1$ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, we found that the equation of the linear relationship represented by the data given in the table above is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - y &= 3k - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the line is expressed in terms of $k$. The idea is that for each possible value of $k$, we get a new line. Let's pick a few values of $k$, determine the corresponding line, and then plot these in the plane.\n\n- If $k=0$, our line is\n$3x - y = 3(0) - 2$\nwhich simplifies as\n$3x - y =-2$.\n- If $k=1$, our line is\n$3x - y = 3(1) - 2$\nwhich simplifies as\n$3x - y = 1$.\n- If $k=2$, our line is\n$3x - y = 4$\n\nAnd so on. We can see that each value of $k$ gives a new line, and in this case, all lines are parallel (since they each have a slope of $3$).\n\n![](images/2c/2c928d7e304f264c60838f5ab29bf2269c96005a74fb9710db8bc23946359206.png)" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186218313 1064 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$k$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$k-1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nEarlier, we found that the equation of the linear relationship represented by the data given in the table above is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - y &= 3k - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur equation is expressed in terms of the parameter $k$. However, if we're given some more information about the line, we can use this to determine the specific value for $k$ that fits the extra data point.\n\nFor example, suppose we know that the $y$-intercept of this line is $10$. This means that the line must pass through the point $(0,10)$. So, substituting $x=0$ and $y =10$ into our line and solving for $k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - y &= 3k - 2 \\\\\n3(0) - 10 &= 3k - 2 \\\\\n0 - 10 &= 3k - 2 \\\\\n- 10 &= 3k - 2 \\\\\n- 8 &= 3k \\\\\n- \\frac{8}{3} &= k \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\nk=-\\dfrac83\n$$\n\nand the equation of our line is\n\n$$\n3x - y = 3\\left(-\\dfrac83\\right) - 2\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n3x - y = -10\n$$" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186218313 1064 "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $k$ | $2$ |\n| $k-1$ | $-1$ |\n\nEarlier, we found that the equation of the linear relationship represented by the data given in the table above is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - y &= 3k - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur equation is expressed in terms of the parameter $k$. However, if we're given some more information about the line, we can use this to determine the specific value for $k$ that fits the extra data point.\n\nFor example, suppose we know that the $y$-intercept of this line is $10$. This means that the line must pass through the point $(0,10)$. So, substituting $x=0$ and $y =10$ into our line and solving for $k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - y &= 3k - 2 \\\\\n3(0) - 10 &= 3k - 2 \\\\\n0 - 10 &= 3k - 2 \\\\\n- 10 &= 3k - 2 \\\\\n- 8 &= 3k \\\\\n- \\frac{8}{3} &= k \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\nk=-\\dfrac83\n$$\n\nand the equation of our line is\n\n$$\n3x - y = 3\\left(-\\dfrac83\\right) - 2\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n3x - y = -10\n$$" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186221115 1071 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Triangle</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Perimeter</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\triangle ABC$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\triangle XYZ$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$72$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTriangles $XYZ$ and $ABC$ are similar, with $\\overline{BC}$ corresponding to $\\overline{YZ}$. The perimeters of the triangles are given in the table above. If $BC = 8$, what is the length of $\\overline{YZ}$?" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186221115 1071 "| Triangle | Perimeter |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\triangle ABC$ | $36$ |\n| $\\triangle XYZ$ | $72$ |\n\nTriangles $XYZ$ and $ABC$ are similar, with $\\overline{BC}$ corresponding to $\\overline{YZ}$. The perimeters of the triangles are given in the table above. If $BC = 8$, what is the length of $\\overline{YZ}$?" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186221160 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = -x - y, \\qquad y\\left(-1\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next rows of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186221160 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = -x - y, \\qquad y\\left(-1\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next rows of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186221160 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(0\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x-y \\\\  & =-0-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-1, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = 1.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{4}+\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-1-\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right)+8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{39}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new} = 1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\left(-\\dfrac{13}{32}\\right) = -\\dfrac{5}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell