 Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z=y'$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{{z}^{2}-4y}{2x}=\\frac{{2}^{2}-4(-1)}{2\\cdot 1}=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+2=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+4=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=6 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{{z}^{2}-4y}{2x}=\\frac{{6}^{2}-4\\cdot 1}{2\\cdot 2}=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot 1=6 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+6=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=6+8=14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ | $6$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $14$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3) \\approx 7$\nand\n$y'(3) = z(3) \\approx 14.$" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186221688 1071 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Triangle</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Perimeter</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$△BCD$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$△PQR$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$64$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTriangles $PQR$ and $BCD$ are similar, with $QR^(―)$ corresponding to $CD^(―)$. The perimeters of the triangles are given in the table above. If $QR = 20$, what is the length of $CD^(―)$?" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186221688 1071 "| Triangle | Perimeter |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $△BCD$ | $16$ |\n| $△PQR$ | $64$ |\n\nTriangles $PQR$ and $BCD$ are similar, with $QR^(―)$ corresponding to $CD^(―)$. The perimeters of the triangles are given in the table above. If $QR = 20$, what is the length of $CD^(―)$?" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186221689 1071 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Triangle</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Perimeter</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$△ABC$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$36$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$△XYZ$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$72$</td>\n</