ll-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, the slope at the point $$\n\\displaystyle (x_\\text{new},y_p) = \\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{13}8\\right) = \\left(1, \\dfrac{13}8\\right)\n$$ estimated by the predictor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{13}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $$\n\\displaystyle (x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)\n$$ is $y' = \\dfrac12.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{39}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}{32}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe know the value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac34 + \\dfrac{13}{32} = \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'_p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac78$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac34$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{13}{32}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{37}{32}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\dfrac{37}{32}.$" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186222480 1064 "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x-2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously initialized with the RK4 method and completed the predictor step of the ABM $2$-step method, resulting in the following values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |  |  |\n\nNow, the slope at the point $$\n\\displaystyle (x_\\text{new},y_p) = \\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{13}8\\right) = \\left(1, \\dfrac{13}8\\right)\n$$ estimated by the predictor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{13}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $$\n\\displaystyle (x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)\n$$ is 