$s>0$ |\n| $t^2\\cos \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{2s(s^2-3\\omega^2)}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^3}$ | $s>0$ |\n| $t\\sinh \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2-\\omega^2)^2}$ | $s>|\\omega|$ |\n| $t^2\\sinh \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{2\\omega(3s^2+\\omega^2)}{(s^2-\\omega^2)^3}$ | $s>|\\omega|$ |\n| $t\\cosh \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{s^2+\\omega^2}{(s^2-\\omega^2)^2}$ | $s>|\\omega|$ |\n| $t^2\\cosh \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{2s(s^2+3\\omega^2)}{(s^2-\\omega^2)^3}$ | $s>|\\omega|$ |\n\nFinally, note that we have a shortcut pattern for exponentials. If\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{kt}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\n\\qquad s>k,\n$$\n\nthen repeated differentiation shows that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^n e^{kt}\\}=\\frac{n!}{(s-k)^{n+1}},\n\\qquad s>k.\n$$\n\nThis pattern is a direct consequence of the smoothness property and provides a fast way to compute many common transforms." 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186222520 1064 "Using the first shifting theorem, we can obtain new Laplace transforms from old ones without performing any additional integration.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThe table below shows some general results we can obtain using the first shifting theorem.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(t)$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\}$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Compare With</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$e^{at} t^n$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathcal L \\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$e^{at}\\sin \\omega t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\omega}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$e^{at}\\cos \\omega t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, each new entry in our table is obtained by taking a known transform and replacing $s$ with $s-a$, and then shifting the domain accordingly." 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186222520 1064 "Using the first shifting theorem, we can obtain new Laplace transforms from old ones without performing any additional integration.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThe table below shows some general results we can obtain using the first shifting theorem.\n\n| $f(t)$ | $F(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\}$ | Compare With |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $e^{at} t^n$ | $\\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}$ | $\\mathcal L \\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ |\n| $e^{at}\\sin \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{\\omega}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$ | $\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ |\n| $e^{at}\\cos \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$ | $\\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ |\n\nSo, each new entry in our table is obtained by taking a known transform and replacing $s$ with $s-a$, and then shifting the domain accordingly." 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186222529 1064 "We now apply the update rules $\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x$ and $\\Delta z = z' \\cdot \\Delta x$ to our system below starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{'} &=\\frac{y+z}{x}+2,\\, && z(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta z$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y,$ and $z,$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{'} & =\\frac{y+z}{x}+2=\\frac{-1+1}{1}+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta z$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAfter several steps, we obtain the following table of values.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z'$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta z$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac72$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\dfrac74$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac72$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\">$\\dfrac52$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac94$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{11}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac94$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y'\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{11}2.\n$$ \n\nNotice that this approach of splitting a second-order ODE into a system of first-order ODEs is not specific to Euler's method. Indeed, we could apply any numerical method component-wise to approximate the solution to a second-order ODE once split into a system of first-order ODEs!" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186222529 1064 "We now apply the update rules $\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x$ and $\\Delta z = z' \\cdot \\Delta x$ to our system below starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{'} &=\\frac{y+z}{x}+2,\\, && z(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y,$ and $z,$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{'} & =\\frac{y+z}{x}+2=\\frac{-1+1}{1}+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nAfter several steps, we obtain the following table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $\\dfrac74$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\dfrac52$ | $\\dfrac94$ |  |  | $\\dfrac{11}2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac94$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y'\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{11}2.\n$$ \n\nNotice that this approach of splitting a second-order ODE into a system of first-order ODEs is not specific to Euler's method. Indeed, we could apply any numerical method component-wise to approximate the solution to a second-order ODE once split into a system of first-order ODEs!" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186223735 1080 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139540373 17592186576411 true] [17592186223735 1080 "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-4$ | $5$ |\n| $-1$ | $5$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ |" 13194139540373 17592186576411 false] [17592186223736 1080 "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-4$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>